---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/peace-conflict-studies/foundational/what-are-peace-and-violence">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the opening lesson of Peace and Conflict Studies, and it assumes no prior
    subject knowledge — only a willingness to have two familiar words taken apart and
    reassembled more precisely. Everything you need is built here.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>distinguish negative peace (no open violence) from positive peace (the conditions that make violence unlikely), and explain why the distinction decides what gets funded and measured;</li>
    <li>use Galtung's three-part typology — direct, structural and cultural violence — and say what each layer adds;</li>
    <li>apply the iceberg diagram and the conflict triangle to see why ceasefires alone so often fail;</li>
    <li>describe how a contested field measures itself: the Global Peace Index, the Positive Peace Index, and the academic conflict datasets;</li>
    <li>state the framework's three strongest criticisms, and the replies its defenders make.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit runs through the lesson: definitions are not pedantry here. How the field
    defines "peace" and "violence" determines what it studies, what it funds and what it
    counts as success — so the definitional argument <em>is</em> the subject's opening move.
  </p>

  <h2>Two words that need surgery</h2>
  <p>
    The Korean peninsula has been "at peace" by one measure since 1953 — no open war — and
    is one of the most heavily armed and divided places on Earth. Which word fits: peace,
    or something else? Now a second case. Two patients catch the same treatable pneumonia.
    One recovers in a country with clinics; the other dies in a country without them. No
    one attacked the second patient. Yet an arrangement — the absence of a clinic, which
    could have been otherwise — shortened a life. Is that violence?
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="bet before you read on — is that violence?" widget="predict" config={pcsClinicPredict} />
  <p>
    These are the questions Peace and Conflict Studies opens with, and the surgery on the
    two words matters because the answers decide the field's entire agenda. If "violence"
    is only what a person does to another person, then the study of peace is the study of
    wars and criminals, and a quiet country with segregated cities, corrupt courts and
    unequal life expectancy is simply at peace. If an arrangement can be violent, the
    agenda widens radically — and so does the job description of a peacebuilder.
  </p>
  <p>
    The field's analytical spine comes from Johan Galtung, the Norwegian sociologist who
    founded the first peace research institute and its first journal. His framework —
    negative versus positive peace, and violence as direct, structural or cultural —
    radicalised the discipline with one move: <em>a country without gunfire can still be
    violent, if its arrangements quietly shorten lives</em>. That single claim redefined
    peacebuilding as something far larger than ceasefire mediation, and it remains the best
    entry point into the field — partly because it is controversial, and the controversy
    teaches.
  </p>
  <p>
    The cards below walk the framework in five steps: two kinds of peace, three kinds of
    violence.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="Galtung's pyramid: peace, direct, structural, cultural" widget="equationStep" config={galtungCfg} />

  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the two halves mirror each other. Negative peace is
    the absence of direct violence; positive peace is the absence of structural violence —
    which is why the peace definitions and the violence typology are one system, not two
    topics. And read the violence cards as a pyramid: cultural violence sits at the base
    because the stories a society tells are what make its direct and structural harms look
    normal, natural or deserved. Remove the base and the top is harder to justify.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the field redefined both of its founding words. Now we take
    the halves in turn, starting with the pair of peaces — because the negative–positive
    distinction explains a pattern the field finds wherever it looks: wars that come back.
  </p>

  <h2>Negative and positive peace</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Negative peace</strong> is the absence of direct violence: the shooting stops.
    It is what most people mean by "peace" and what most diplomacy delivers — ceasefires,
    armistices, the end of open hostilities. It is genuinely valuable, and it is
    insufficient, because a ceasefire can freeze a conflict as easily as end it: the guns
    silence while the grievances, the displaced populations and the armed actors all
    remain, waiting. Korea's armistice has "held" since 1953; nobody calls the peninsula
    peace. Northern Ireland's Troubles paused and restarted repeatedly before 1998, and the
    Good Friday Agreement's durability came not from stopping the shooting but from
    rebuilding the conditions — power-sharing, decommissioning, cross-border institutions —
    that made resumption unlikely.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Positive peace</strong> is the presence of the conditions under which violence
    is unlikely: functioning and fair institutions, equitable access to opportunity,
    freedom of information, integration across social divides. The distinction explains
    the relapse pattern: a substantial share of countries emerging from civil war fall
    back into it within a decade, and the risk concentrates where negative peace was never
    followed by positive peace — disarmament without reintegration, elections without
    institutions, ceasefires without grievance settlement. The definitions are not
    academic; they decide what gets funded and measured. If peace is merely the absence of
    shooting, a ceasefire counts as success and its collapse is mysterious. If peace
    requires positive conditions, the ceasefire is only the beginning, and the field's job
    is to identify, build and measure those conditions.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "positive peace is just a nicer word for stability."
  It is nearly the opposite — stability can be imposed by repression (a violent "peace"),
  while positive peace is specified by justice and functioning institutions. Authoritarian
  order scores as negative peace with structural violence intact; the typology is built
  precisely to keep those two from being confused.</p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="file each feature — positive peace or imposed stability" widget="sort-buckets" config={pcsStabilitySort} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "if negative peace is insufficient, ceasefires and
  armistices are pointless." Learners who meet Galtung often overcorrect into dismissing
  ceasefire diplomacy. The framework says no such thing: stopping the shooting saves lives
  now and is the precondition for everything else. The claim is about sufficiency, not
  worth — negative peace is the necessary first half of a job, and the collapse comes when
  it is treated as the whole job.</p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="true or false — the ceasefire overcorrection" widget="judge" config={pcsCeasefireJudge} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> two peaces — absence of violence versus presence of the
    conditions against relapse. The next section supplies the other half of the mirror:
    what the framework means by violence, in three layers.
  </p>

  <h2>Direct, structural and cultural violence</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Direct violence</strong> is the visible layer: an identifiable actor doing
    identifiable harm — war, assault, murder, torture. It is what news covers, what law
    punishes, and what most people mean by violence. It is also, in scale, the smallest
    layer: epidemiologists who try to count lives lost by cause find that preventable
    death from arrangements — absent clinics, contaminated water, famine amid plenty —
    dwarfs death by weapons.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Structural violence</strong> is harm built into arrangements, with no actor and
    no moment of harm — and Galtung's example (1969) remains the clearest: two people die
    of the same treatable pneumonia; one in a country with clinics, one without. No one
    pulled a trigger, but the arrangement shortened a life, and it would have shortened a
    statistically predictable number of lives — which is what makes the concept analytical
    rather than rhetorical. Epidemiology now quantifies this as excess mortality; the field
    reads it as violence-by-structure and asks the framework's sharpest question: who
    benefits from the arrangement persisting? The concept's power is that it keeps visible
    what comfortable societies prefer to dissolve into background statistics — the
    different life expectancies across neighbourhoods of the same city are, on this
    analysis, a peace-and-conflict fact, not just a public-health one.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Cultural violence</strong> (Galtung's 1990 addition) is the legitimating
    layer: the stories, symbols, ideologies and media frames that make the other two forms
    look normal, natural or deserved. Dehumanising propaganda precedes genocides; "they
    are not really like us" narratives soften discrimination into policy; and specific
    cultural practices — honour codes that treat women as family property, dowry logic,
    child marriage — teach that certain harms are proper. The scale is documented: the
    United Nations' 1993 Declaration on the Elimination of Violence against Women defined
    violence against women as any act of gender-based violence causing physical, sexual or
    psychological harm, in public or private life; World Health Organization research
    suggests roughly one woman in three worldwide has been beaten, raped or abused, with
    rates highest where norms and laws keep women subordinate. Cultural violence is also
    where the framework is most contested — critics note that marking a culture "violent"
    risks its own kind of prejudice, and that the category can blur into disapproval of
    beliefs rather than analysis of harm. The careful version is the honest one: cultural
    violence names <em>specific stories used to legitimate specific harms</em>, not
    cultures as wholes.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="pair each violence layer with its signature" widget="match" config={pcsViolenceMatch} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "calling structures violent is mere metaphor." The
  framework's defence is criterion-based: violence means avoidable harm that shortens or
  damages life. If that harm is inflicted by an actor, it is direct; if by an arrangement
  that could be otherwise, it is structural. Whether the extension of the word is wise is
  a fair debate — but the definition is doing analytical work, not decoration.</p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "violence is only what breaks bones or draws blood."
  Everyday and legal language already concede otherwise: threats, coercion and sustained
  psychological abuse are recognised as violence — the UN definition above explicitly
  includes psychological harm, whether occurring in public or private life. The framework
  extends the same logic one step further, from harm done by a person to harm built into
  an arrangement. It is a widening of an ordinary concept, not an invention of a strange
  one.</p>

  <h2>Conflict analysis: the iceberg and the triangle</h2>
  <p>
    The definitional work feeds the field's first practical tool: conflict analysis. The
    classic <strong>iceberg diagram</strong> teaches that a conflict's visible behaviour —
    the fighting, the protest, the breakdown — floats on layers beneath it: beneath the
    behaviour sit the <strong>attitudes</strong> (fear, grievance, dehumanising narratives
    — cultural violence's territory), and beneath the attitudes sit the
    <strong>contradictions</strong>: the structural incompatibilities (contested land,
    excluded groups, economic imbalance) that generate the conflict in the first place.
    Galtung's own <strong>conflict triangle</strong> names the same structure — behaviour,
    attitude, contradiction — and its warning is operational: resolve only the behaviour
    (a ceasefire) while attitudes and contradictions persist, and the conflict regenerates;
    the field's term for this is <strong>latent conflict</strong>, the iceberg underwater,
    waiting. Positive peace, on this analysis, is simply a society whose triangle is
    managed — whose contradictions are addressable through institutions rather than
    violence. The iceberg is why the field distrusts quick wins: any peace process that
    never goes below the waterline is a schedule, not a settlement.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="sequence the iceberg's layers" widget="order" config={pcsIcebergOrder} />

  <h2>Measuring peace</h2>
  <p>
    Modern peace research quantifies what it studies — precisely because "peace" is
    contested and measurement disciplines the definitions. The <strong>Global Peace
    Index</strong> (Institute for Economics and Peace, published annually since 2007)
    scores about 160 countries on 23 indicators across three domains: the level of
    societal safety and security, the extent of ongoing domestic and international
    conflict, and the degree of militarisation — mixing hard data (battle deaths,
    refugees, homicides, imprisoned populations) with qualitative expert assessments. Its
    companion <strong>Positive Peace Index</strong> measures the institutional conditions —
    well-functioning government, low corruption, equitable distribution, free flow of
    information, high human capital, acceptance of others' rights — operationalising
    Galtung's positive peace into eight pillars that the index's own analysis links to
    resilience and relapse risk.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="pair each measure with its method" widget="match" config={pcsIndexMatch} />
  <p>
    The academic datasets run deeper and longer. The <strong>Uppsala Conflict Data
    Program</strong> (UCDP) has coded armed conflicts since 1946 with strict thresholds (a
    "war" requires 1,000 battle-related deaths in a year; smaller conflicts are coded
    separately), making cross-country comparison and time-series analysis possible; the
    <strong>Correlates of War</strong> project has coded wars since 1816; casualty
    archives and event datasets track violence at fine grain. This is the field's
    <strong>empirical turn</strong>: the numbers do not settle the definitional debates —
    but they expose patterns the definitions must reckon with, such as the post-1945
    decline of interstate war relative to civil conflict, or the concentration of conflict
    relapse in low-positive-peace societies.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A ceasefire holds for two years, then the war resumes. Using the iceberg, say what went unresolved — and which kind of peace was never built.</li>
    <li>Classify each as direct, structural or cultural violence: (a) a massacre; (b) a radio serial that portrays an ethnic group as vermin; (c) a district whose clinics closed, doubling infant mortality.</li>
    <li>Why does the field measure peace with indices and datasets if the definitions are contested?</li>
    <li>An authoritarian state has low crime and no civil war but jails journalists and has a twenty-year life-expectancy gap between its richest and poorest regions. Negative peace, positive peace, both, or neither?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Only the behaviour was resolved; the attitudes
    (grievance, dehumanising narratives) and contradictions (land, exclusion) stayed
    underwater as latent conflict — negative peace was achieved, positive peace was never
    built. (2) (a) direct — actor and harm visible; (b) cultural — the legitimating story,
    not the deed itself; (c) structural — harm from an arrangement, no shooter required.
    (3) Measurement disciplines the definitions and exposes patterns debate must reckon
    with — relapse rates, the interstate-versus-civil shift — turning contested words into
    comparable data. (4) Negative peace (no open violence) without positive peace: the
    repression is direct violence's quieter cousin, and the life-expectancy gap is
    textbook structural violence — the typology is designed to catch exactly this case.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Negative peace is the absence of direct violence; positive peace is the presence of the conditions that make violence unlikely — and only the second prevents relapse.</li>
    <li>Violence, in this field, is avoidable harm that shortens or damages life: direct (an actor), structural (an arrangement), cultural (the legitimating story).</li>
    <li>The three forms form a pyramid — the stories at the base make the harms at the top look normal or deserved.</li>
    <li>The iceberg (behaviour, attitudes, contradictions) explains why ceasefires alone regenerate conflict; latent conflict is the part below the waterline.</li>
    <li>The Global Peace Index, the Positive Peace Index, UCDP (since 1946) and Correlates of War (since 1816) turn contested definitions into comparable data.</li>
    <li>The framework's controversies — expansion, agency, comparability — are part of the toolkit: knowing where a definition breaks is part of knowing it.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The next lesson asks the causal question this one has deliberately parked: why wars
    happen — at the level of individuals, groups and states, and through nationalism,
    religion, resources and territory. After that, the structural and cultural violence
    you met here get lessons of their own (poverty, media and language, gender and
    conflict), and the later units turn to the positive-peace project itself: how peace is
    negotiated, kept, and how justice is done after atrocity. Every one of them begins
    with the definitions you now hold.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The framework's critics sharpen it.</strong> Three objections recur. First,
    <em>expansion</em>: if violence includes structures, the concept risks covering
    everything and explaining nothing — the reply is the avoidability criterion, which
    keeps the concept bounded even if bounded debates remain. Second, <em>agency</em>:
    structural violence names no perpetrator, which troubles both law (who is tried?) and
    politics (who is responsible?) — the framework answers with the benefits question
    rather than a culprit, which is coherent but unsatisfying in courtrooms. Third,
    <em>comparability</em>: how much structural violence equals how much direct violence?
    There is no exchange rate; the typology clarifies categories, not trade-offs. Peace
    researchers who hold all three objections in view use the framework as a lens for
    generating hypotheses and questions, not a formula for verdicts.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="walk the three critics" widget="branch" config={pcsCriticsBranch} />
  <p>
    <strong>The definitions have already shaped practice.</strong> Peacebuilding
    architecture — the UN's peacebuilding commission and fund, the World Bank's
    conflict-and-development work, the humanitarian–development–peace "triple nexus" —
    operationalises positive peace: after a ceasefire, the intervention portfolio includes
    institution-building, reconciliation processes, economic inclusion and security-sector
    reform, because the relapse data says those are the levers. Post-conflict economics
    (Paul Collier's "conflict trap" analysis) and reconciliation practice (truth
    commissions, which treat cultural violence directly by re-authorising the stories a
    society tells) are the framework's children.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Conflict sensitivity closes the introductory loop.</strong> Once peace and
    violence have been redefined this broadly, an uncomfortable implication follows for
    everyone who acts in conflict zones — aid agencies, companies, peacekeepers: every
    intervention enters the conflict system and changes it. The field's answer is
    <strong>conflict sensitivity</strong>, crystallised in Mary B. Anderson's "Do No Harm"
    framework: analyse the dividers and connectors before acting — who is being empowered,
    whose grievance is being fed, which local rivalry your resource distribution is about
    to fund — so that well-intentioned programmes stop accidentally doing harm. The
    classic warning cases are structural in Galtung's exact sense: relief routed through
    one ethnic group's networks, a reconstruction contract that employed one side's
    militias, a new well that redrew whose land was worth fighting for. Positive peace,
    applied, means designing interventions that connect rather than divide — and that
    habit of second-order thinking is the first professional skill this definitional work
    exists to produce.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="drag between neutral aid and conflict sensitivity" widget="compare" config={pcsAidCompare} />

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Galtung, "Violence, Peace, and Peace Research" (1969); Barash &amp; Webel, <em>Peace and Conflict Studies</em>; Global Peace Index methodology. Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>
